00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.370
It’s 5 o’clock.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.499
You just finished a shift in your small Winsconsin
dairy town.

00:00:17.499 --> 00:00:20.199
You’re on an evening stroll in New York.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:22.650
Dinner is calling in a St. Louis suburb.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:27.509
Then, you step into the grocery store and
suddenly you’re transported somewhere entirely

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:28.829
different:

00:00:28.829 --> 00:00:34.210
Here the parking lots are never big enough,
the lines, always long, and picking Pita chips

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.510
somehow feels adventurous.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:41.469
Here you’re sophisticated - you appreciate
the difference between mezzelune and non-mezzelune

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:43.199
ravioli.

00:00:43.199 --> 00:00:45.809
What is this strange, alternate universe?

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:48.410
And why is everyone so scandalously friendly?

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.170
You’ve just entered the 15,000 square-foot
Californian Embassy.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.359
Or, more specifically, your neighborhood Trader
Joe’s.

00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:03.250
Like its motherland, the nation of California,
Trader Joe’s does everything a bit differently.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:09.590
It holds no sales, offers no coupons, has
no loyalty program, no self-checkout, almost

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:14.570
no advertising, no online ordering, and collects
no customer data.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:22.440
You’ll either find its 25-cent individually-sold
bananas quirky and charming or endlessly obnoxious.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.920
In case you’re unfamiliar, Trader Joe’s
is a popular American specialty-grocery store

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.780
- meaning you’ll find things like the Veggie
Burger with Almond Butter Turmeric Slaw, produce,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.560
and snacks, but few everyday essentials like
batteries or toothpicks.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.649
For that reason, the average shopper makes
routine visits but in addition to their normal,

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:43.210
big-box grocery store.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:49.250
It’s devotees appreciate its cheap and wide
variety of wine and diet or food-restriction-friendly

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:53.079
choices, like vegan, kosher, and gluten-free
foods.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:58.079
It’s perhaps the only grocery store with
a cult-following, Facebook fan groups, and

00:01:58.079 --> 00:01:59.750
employees for life.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.390
Clearly it’s doing something very different.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.360
The first thing you can’t help but notice
as you enter a Trader Joe’s, is its elaborate

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.679
tropical and nautical theme.

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:14.730
It doesn’t feel like an ordinary store because
its aisles aren’t repetitive rows of similar-looking

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:15.730
items.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:22.180
Rather, it’s more akin to a coherently-designed
theme park ride - unsurprising given its inspiration.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.280
Before opening the chain, its founder Joe
Coulombe had been on Disneyland’s jungle

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.280
ride.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.310
The titular Joe in supermarket lore, is a
sailor on the high seas, bringing exotic foods

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.690
from faraway places.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.519
Managers wear bright, casual Hawaiian shirts,
sometimes with leis, and are officially designated

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:40.819
‘Captains’.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.420
Assistant managers are ‘First Mates’,
and regular employees ‘Crew’.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.330
The walls, aisles, and floor are decorated
in wooden tiki patterns.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.459
Between them you’ll find Trader Joe’s
second surprise and one of the key ingredients

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:58.799
to its success - there are almost no branded
items.

00:02:58.799 --> 00:03:03.870
Around 80% of all its products carry the Trader
Joe’s label, with a unique package design,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.730
and creative, often quirky, names.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:11.700
The average shopper may see only one logo
and one, consistent design from the entrance

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.450
to the exit - in sharp contrast with the average
grocery store aisle, where each product independently

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.790
fights for your attention.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:24.540
And yet, in what might surprise even the most
frequent customers, Trader Joe’s owns no

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.540
factories.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.750
It does not cook, build, or create its recipes.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:34.560
Instead, the company approaches an existing
brand, or vice versa, and strikes a deal.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.840
Trader Joe’s gets to put its name on high-quality,
well-liked, and pre-tested foods.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.439
The manufacturer, meanwhile, gets a new source
of income from a customer who will buy regularly,

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:51.129
in large quantities, and pay on-delivery,
rather than charging slotting fees like most

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:52.129
grocers.

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:57.739
Both agree to secrecy - legally obligating
one another never to admit to the arrangement.

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:00.720
There are, however, some clever ways around
this.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.450
When the American Food and Drug Administration
recalls a product, its manufacturers often

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.690
release public lists of all the stores and
labels under which it’s sold.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:15.019
The blog Eater has used Freedom of Information
Act requests to obtain these lists and figure

00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:17.670
out many of Trader Joe’s suppliers.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:23.110
While some of its products are genuinely exclusive,
many are very slight modifications, different

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:29.620
proportions, or even exact recreations of
products available elsewhere - meaning, awkwardly,

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.510
you can compare prices directly.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:36.780
It’s White Cheddar Macaroni, for example,
was discovered to be identical to Annie’s

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.930
branded version, but, at Trader Joe’s, 50%
cheaper.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:46.421
Its Veggie Sticks are, except for a few ingredients,
the same as Good Health Natural Foods, despite

00:04:46.421 --> 00:04:48.660
being less than half the cost.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:54.110
It also sells products suspiciously similar
to Wonderful Pistachios, Naked Juice, Frito-Lay

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.230
chips, and Stauffer’s animal crackers.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.910
This private-label strategy, as it’s known,
is not unique.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:06.780
Whole Foods has a similar brand called 365,
and Costco has its Kirkland Signature.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.160
Trader Joe’s, though, has selected products
and positioned them well enough that shoppers

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.250
see its brand as Trader Joe’s.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:19.111
In other words, customers feel they’re shopping
at a premium retailer like Whole Foods, while

00:05:19.111 --> 00:05:23.360
getting generic-brand prices - the best of
both worlds.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.460
The downside of this strategy is that products
and relationships come and go.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:33.650
Because each store sells only about 4,000
unique products, compared to the average grocery

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:38.370
store’s 40,000, it meticulously manages
inventory.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.250
Being a customer means constantly being disappointed
as your favorite items are discontinued or

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:45.010
go out of season.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:49.800
This tight inventory management is also why
items sell out so frequently.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.270
Trader Joe’s purchases directly from manufacturers
and its stores have small back-rooms, meaning

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:01.090
trucks deliver new stock every day and predicting
demand is a difficult art to perfect.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:07.120
The company began private-labeling back in
1972, 5 years after opening the first store

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.810
in Pasadena, California, which is still open
today.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:16.900
Since then, the company has expanded to over
500 stores across 42 states plus D.C., with

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.320
the strange omission of Hawaii, considering
its tropical theme.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:26.010
Despite this growth, most would say it’s
maintained that signature neighborhood-feel.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:31.910
Each of its 500 stores hires artists to hand-draw
its signs and reflect the local culture.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:37.100
The brand hearkens back to a simpler time
- one with less technology - one where staff

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.350
were knowledgeable and treated customers as
friends.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:46.600
It feels, nostalgic - familiar, somehow, even
to those who’ve never lived in such an era.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:52.660
Its checkout stations have no conveyor belts,
scales, or TV screens, and, instead of a PA

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:58.430
system, employees use a literal bell - one
ding to call extra cashiers, two for general

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.850
assistance, and three to notify the manager.

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:06.490
Free samples are handed-out liberally, and
each store makes its own merchandising decisions

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.180
- catering to local tastes.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.040
Perhaps most important, though, are its staff.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:18.070
Trader Joe’s hires not based on skill but
personality - it exclusively recruits smart,

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.400
energetic, conversationalist extroverts.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.620
Here, the customer is always right - some
have reported successfully returning dead

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.070
plants or empty packaging without a receipt.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:34.090
The crew gladly helps carry groceries to cars
and are expressly told to drop everything

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:36.860
when approached by a customer for any reason.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:41.690
They will always lead you to a product, not
just tell you which aisle it’s on.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:46.230
In fact, to encourage these interactions,
staff are told to stock products throughout

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:51.060
the day, where they will be in close contact
with customers, rather than before or after

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.580
the opening hours.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.280
And, because these tasks are both physically
and socially draining, workers frequently

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.170
rotate between them.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:03.570
The job of greeting customers at the door,
the ‘helmsman’ in Joe’s parlance, is

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.190
assigned for no more than 1-hour at a time.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:09.370
And working the register, at most, two hours.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:15.060
Together, all these quirks place Trader Joe’s
firmly in an unofficial family of oddly-similar,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:21.030
West-Coast at-heart brands with personality,
along with companies like In-N-Out and Costco.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:27.340
All three have a borderline cult following,
truly low prices, a reflective, top-down management

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:33.560
style, and, treat employees not as expendable
objects but assets to be nurtured.

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:38.530
Each appeals to the kind of customer who has
money but chooses to be frugal.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:43.530
Someone who enjoys the comfort of a small,
local establishment while, at the same time,

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.520
feeling a bit adventurous.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:50.330
All were founded by charismatic figures, in
Southern California, and remain secretive

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.490
about their internal workings.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:57.800
Trader Joe’s headquarters in Monrovia, California
is tellingly nondescript, and the company

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.930
rarely accepts requests for interviews.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:06.950
Although Trader Joe’s founder Joe Coulombe
died in February, his legacy is undeniable.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:12.440
He built a place people look forward to shopping
at, a place where everyone - customers and

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.420
employees are treated with respect.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:19.950
Some would say it’s largely a facade - that
its food may not be as healthy as one would

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:24.890
assume, that the company is not the pinnacle
of environmentalism it would have you believe

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:29.250
and that a national chain, by definition,
can’t be local.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:34.030
If one is being totally honest, the store
has a slightly snobby feel to it.

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:38.560
But whether or not you’re a fan, you can’t
help but admire its ingenuity: building a

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:43.510
brand that truly transcends a mere name, logo,
and ‘mission statement’.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:49.000
For better or worse, Trader Joe’s succeeds
because it exports California.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:55.210
No matter who you are, or where you are, here
everyone is friendly, everything is colorful,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:56.650
and life is simple.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:01.940
I don’t normally like talking about unreleased
videos, but one that’s been on my list for

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:06.050
some time is another secretive, well-liked
company: Lego!

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:10.810
I’m not sure if this video will ever see
the light of day, but, in the meantime, I

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:15.140
really recommend watching “The Secret World
of Lego” documentary on CuriosityStream.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:20.250
It’s full of interesting stories, like,
for example, did you know there’s a coast

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.230
in the UK where Lego pieces occasionally wash
ashore?

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:29.240
Because you’re watching this video, I strongly
suspect you’d enjoy the technology, science,

